ralph/codex-output/iteration-000013-20260518-171620.log:998:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED COUNTER table whose ONLY public mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING + SELECT...FOR UPDATE primitive (LockUsage), with NO per-row Update/Delete, NO version column, NO service_id pointer, and a database-managed used_value mutated by an EXTERNAL code path (quota.Checker, not the repository), set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only** (BE-0453 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0451 (quota_policies — INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in four load-bearing ways: (a) the second-call byte-identity proof must anchor updated_at TOO — ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING is not an UPDATE and the set_updated_at trigger must not fire (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE with even a no-op SET would silently bump updated_at and a missing anchor would let that slip past); (b) the peer-row proof is a SAME-TENANT-DIFFERENT-RESOURCE check (UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) isolates rows by resource within a tenant); (c) the row-already-exists-with-non-zero-value leg has no policies analog — pre-seed via raw SQL at a sentinel value (e.g. used_value=17, id=`qu_seed_<resource>`) and prove BOTH the seeded id AND the seeded used_value survive a LockUsage call (this is the ONLY way to prove the discarded INSERT's minted prefix id does not replace the row's id — a regression to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE SET id=EXCLUDED.id would be a catastrophic data-loss path that typed reader projections would not catch); (d) the DOMAIN-violation test casts an arbitrary string to the typed Resource (e.g. `store.QuotaResource("not-a-resource")`) to bypass Go's compile-time check and exercise the DOMAIN quota_resource CHECK at the database layer — surfacing as yerr.CodeConflict through mapWriteError. The 8-new-test shape (row-shape, second-call-byte-identity, peer-row-byte-identity, persisted-used-value, unknown-org-Conflict, unknown-resource-Conflict, tx-rollback, nil-tx-Internal) leaves the pre-existing TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageCreatesAndReturnsCounter in quota_repository_test.go alone as the load-bearing two-call-idempotency probe. Helper symbols introduced: `lockUsageOrFail`, `loadQuotaUsageRow`, `countQuotaUsageRowsForOrg`, `rawQuotaUsageRow`, `quotaUsageTxRollbackSentinel`. Reused symbols from sibling files: `seedOrg` (schema_test.go), `seedQuotaUsage` (usage_test.go), `newStore` (store_test.go) — note `seedQuotaUsage` is the ONLY way to set used_value to a non-zero value through a test fixture because the repository surface has no equivalent (LockUsage cannot increment).
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000013-20260518-171620.log:1112:- Real-quota concurrent oversubscription integration test (BE-0323 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_quota_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0324..0338 — every other quota-dimension PRD story that needs to prove "parallel requests cannot oversubscribe the quota" against the dimension's owning Service.Create call): one Go test, t.Parallel, against testutil.RequireMigratedDB; wires a REAL *quota.Checker over a REAL *store.QuotaRepository + quota.StaticPlanResolver, and a REAL *store.<Resource>Service + REAL *store.AuditRepository, with the only fakes being the Authorizer and JobEnqueuer (recordingAuthorizer / recordingJobs from projectservice_test.go) which always succeed because their failure-path stories are covered elsewhere. Six load-bearing rules: (1) pre-seed quota_usage with used_value=0 for (org, dimension) BEFORE the race so the SELECT FOR UPDATE inside Checker.LockUsage acquires an existing row rather than racing inserts — the lock contract under test is row-lock serialisation, not row-creation; (2) seed quota_policies as scope_kind='plan_default' (not 'organization' override) so the plan-default fallback path inside QuotaRepository.EffectiveLimit is the one exercised — the org-override path has its own dedicated repository test (TestQuotaRepositoryEffectiveLimitPrefersOrgOverride); (3) launch attempts > limit goroutines (BE-0323 uses 20 attempts / limit 5 — 4:1 ratio gives a comfortable margin against any future pool-size or scheduling change without inflating runtime past ~0.5s); each goroutine generates its OWN canonical resource id via domain.MustNewID + a unique canonical slug fmt.Sprintf("p-%02d", i) so a slug conflict cannot disguise itself as a quota rejection; (4) assert successes == limit AND rejections == attempts - limit exactly, NOT >= / <= — the lock contract guarantees an exact split, and a "successes >= limit / <= attempts" range would silently let a regression through; (5) for EVERY rejection, assert all five ExceededDetail fields (Resource, Limit, Requested=1, Reserved==limit, Current==0) because at the moment the lock yields to a losing transaction every winning reservation is already committed — anything less than Reserved==limit means the lock did not serialise — and assert the diagnostic string never echoes any caller input (the i-indexed slug, display name, request id, or correlation id) so a future logging path cannot turn the error into a content channel; (6) post-race assertions cover three independent surfaces of the same invariant: raw count of projects rows for the org (must equal limit), raw count of active quota_reservations rows for the dimension (must equal limit), AND quotaRepo.SumActiveReservations through Store.Read (must equal limit) — the third surface catches a future regression where the table content diverges from the repository's view. Fake-call assertions pin step order at the unit-of-work level: authz.calls == attempts (every transaction reaches authorization) but jobs.calls == limit (rejected transactions never reach the JobEnqueuer because quota fails first and rolls back the whole tx). The dimension constant comes from the public store.QuotaResource* surface (BE-0323 uses store.QuotaResourceProjects) so a future rename surfaces as a compile error rather than a flaky test. NOT applicable to quota dimensions whose enforcement seam lives outside the *Service.Create unit of work (e.g. cpu_millicores / memory_mb / storage_gb where the dimension is summed across child resources — those need a different shape that exercises the dimension's actual enforcement gate). The CapDeploy concurrent_deployments dimension is the load-bearing precedent for "summed across rows" enforcement (DeploymentService.Create at deployment_service.go:209+691) and would clone this template by swapping the Service + dimension while keeping the six rules intact.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000013-20260518-171620.log:1170:- Quota schema (0006_quota_policy_schema): a closed enum reused across tables is a Postgres DOMAIN over text + CHECK (here quota_resource over 14 dimensions, quota_enforcement_mode over hard/soft/metered/disabled); DROP DOMAIN goes in the down migration after the tables. quota_policies holds one limit per (scope, resource) where a row is EITHER a plan_default (text plan, no plans-table FK yet) OR an organization override — enforced by one table CHECK + two partial unique indexes; the checker resolves "org override else plan default". quota_usage is one counter row per (org, resource); quota_reservations are short-lived (amount>0, status active->committed|released|expired, expires_at, settled_at tied to non-active status by CHECK) and expose UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as a composite-FK target; usage_events is append-only (no updated_at/trigger, signed delta) and links reservations via a tenant-safe composite FK. An optional cross-table link inside a tenant uses a plain composite FK (MATCH SIMPLE) — NOT ON DELETE SET NULL, which would null the NOT NULL organization_id.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000013-20260518-171620.log:1222:- Per-subtype quota dimension on the services Create unit of work (BE-0326 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/serviceservice.go ServiceService.Create + internal/controlplane/store/service_quota_applications_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0327 compose_stacks, BE-0328 databases — every per-subtype dimension whose denominator is a SUBSET of the services table filtered by Kind): the create unit of work calls svc.quota.Reserve TWICE in the same *Tx for a service whose kind consumes a per-subtype dimension — first the existing QuotaResourceServices reservation (counts every service), then a conditional `if service.Kind == ServiceKindXxx { Reserve(QuotaResourceXxx) }` AFTER it and BEFORE the services.Insert. Order matters: the services reservation locks first so a tenant who exhausts the services dimension cannot acquire the per-subtype counter at all; the per-subtype reservation locks second so a tenant who exhausts ONLY the per-subtype dimension still gets a typed apierr.QuotaExceeded whose ExceededDetail.Resource names the per-subtype dimension specifically. Both reservations roll back together on any later failure (Insert, Enqueue, audit Append). Test pattern (clone of BE-0325): TWO quota_usage rows + TWO quota_policies seeded (per-subtype TIGHT at 5, services LOOSE at attempts*2 so the per-subtype dimension is the boundary that bites), assert detail.Resource == QuotaResourceXxx, assert BOTH active reservation tables == success count (because every winning Create reserved one of each). A second non-concurrent test exhausts the per-subtype dimension then proves a sibling-kind Create (compose / database for BE-0326) still succeeds — pins the per-subtype boundary as a regression barrier against a future refactor that drops the Kind guard. BE-0326 already lands the kind-guard inside ServiceService.Create at the same line where Reserve(services) returns nil; BE-0327 and BE-0328 each add ONE more conditional Reserve call right below the BE-0326 block (Kind == ServiceKindCompose / ServiceKindDatabase) and ONE more test file cloned from service_quota_applications_concurrent_test.go with the dimension constant + Kind constant + sibling-kind sanity-success cases swapped. NOT applicable to dimensions that span MULTIPLE service kinds with a non-trivial denominator (cpu_millicores / memory_mb / storage_gb — those count a numeric column on every kind and belong to a different per-numeric-resource pattern still to be designed).
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000013-20260518-171620.log:1223:- Per-Kind quota facet on environments (BE-0330 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environmentservice.go + migrate/migrations/0025_environment_kind.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/environment_quota_preview_environments_concurrent_test.go): structural template for ANY future per-Kind environment quota dimension (analog of the BE-0326/0327/0328 services per-Kind dimensions, now ported to the environments table). The FIRST per-Kind environment story lands the seam: a `kind` text column on environments with NOT NULL DEFAULT 'standard' and a CHECK constraint mirroring the closed Go enum one-to-one; a partial index on (organization_id, project_id) WHERE kind = '<minority-kind>' for the future sweeper; a `Kind` field on Environment, CreateEnvironmentInput, and CloneEnvironmentInput; a validateEnvironmentKind helper that normalises blank to the default member so legacy callers stay green; a closed `EnvironmentKind*` const set; and a conditional Reserve(QuotaResource<KindFacet>) on BOTH EnvironmentService.Create AND EnvironmentService.Clone (environments are the only resource with two create-shape entrypoints, both must be gated together). Subsequent per-Kind environment quota stories ship test-only — the seam is in place after BE-0330. The Reserve gates run in the SAME *Tx as Reserve(QuotaResourceEnvironments) and the desired-state write, so a tenant that exhausts either dimension cannot half-write an environment row whose other dimension passed. The default-on-blank Go validator MUST match the SQL DEFAULT so the migration is forward-compatible with in-process callers that predate the Kind field.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000013-20260518-171620.log:1227:- Quota-dimension story whose call site is NOT yet wired (BE-0337 api_keys pattern, internal/controlplane/store/apikeyservice.go + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0027_quota_resource_api_keys.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/apikey_quota_api_keys_concurrent_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go; structural template for BE-0338 members and ANY future dimension story whose owning Service.Create predates the QuotaReserver port — applies whenever a dimension story is the FIRST to wire its service-layer Reserve, distinct from the BE-0331..BE-0336 'call site already there' siblings whose owning unit-of-work already reserves the dimension). The minimum atomic delivery is FOUR change axes: (1) closed-set expansion — a new `NNNN_quota_resource_<name>.{up,down}.sql` migration that follows the BE-0326 backup_schedules template verbatim (DO block looking up the inline DOMAIN CHECK by `contypid = 'quota_resource'::regtype AND contype = 'c'` so the migration is robust to any Postgres-assigned constraint name, then `ALTER DOMAIN quota_resource ADD CONSTRAINT quota_resource_check CHECK (VALUE IN (...))` with the prior set + the new label; the .down.sql is structurally identical and narrows back to the prior set, accepting that any row carrying the new label fails the restored CHECK — that is the correct behaviour, silently dropping rows would lose audit-relevant state); plus the matching `QuotaResource<Name>` constant + membership-set entry in internal/controlplane/store/quota.go; plus updating the `valid` slice in internal/controlplane/store/quota_internal_test.go::TestQuotaResourceValid which pins `len(valid) == len(quotaResources)` — the test is a sealing-trap that catches a forgotten membership-set entry but ALSO fires on a forgotten valid-slice entry; both must change together. (2) Constructor port widening — the existing service struct grows a `quota QuotaReserver` field, the constructor parameter list grows the same port, the nil-guard switch grows a `case quota == nil` arm placed BETWEEN the last existing repo arm and the audit arm (the convention is store → parent repos → own repo → authz? → quota → jobs? → audit), and the Create unit of work grows a `svc.quota.Reserve(ctx, tx, key.OrganizationID, string(QuotaResource<Name>))` call placed AFTER every existence/parent-lookup check and BEFORE the desired-state Insert so a 404 on the parent is never disguised as a quota rejection AND a row-level conflict (slug, prefix, unique constraint) rolls the reservation back with the rest of the unit of work. (3) Constructor call-site fanout — three call sites change: cmd/yalla-api/main.go grows a `<name>Quota := noopQuotaReserver{}` placeholder (the real quota.Checker adapter is wired separately by a cross-cutting plan-resolver story; placeholder mirrors the projectQuota / environmentQuota / serviceQuota convention exactly), the in-package `package store` constructor-nil test grows a `nopQuotaReserver{}` local + a new `{nil quota, ...}` case wedged BETWEEN the last existing nil case and the audit case (positional ordering must mirror the constructor's nil-guard switch), and the `package store_test` integration-test helper grows the shared `&recordingQuota{}` (defined in projectservice_test.go — not a local `noopQuotaReserver{}`, which is only visible to cmd/yalla-api; the store_test package has its OWN allow-all fake hierarchy and the convention is to reuse `recordingQuota` for happy-path constructors). (4) Concurrent integration test — clone of the BE-0336 concurrent_deployments test with five structural simplifications when the owning service has no Authorizer / JobEnqueuer ports (api_keys is pure Yalla identity with no Dokploy mirror, so APIKeyService takes neither): no `atomicAuthorizer` / `atomicJobs` fakes need to be defined or counted; no `authz.calls == attempts` and no `jobs.calls == successes` assertions; the per-goroutine input loop calls `auth.Generate()`