ralph/codex-output/iteration-000055-20260519-014331.log:1445:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED COUNTER table whose ONLY public mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING + SELECT...FOR UPDATE primitive (LockUsage), with NO per-row Update/Delete, NO version column, NO service_id pointer, and a database-managed used_value mutated by an EXTERNAL code path (quota.Checker, not the repository), set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only** (BE-0453 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0451 (quota_policies — INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in four load-bearing ways: (a) the second-call byte-identity proof must anchor updated_at TOO — ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING is not an UPDATE and the set_updated_at trigger must not fire (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE with even a no-op SET would silently bump updated_at and a missing anchor would let that slip past); (b) the peer-row proof is a SAME-TENANT-DIFFERENT-RESOURCE check (UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) isolates rows by resource within a tenant); (c) the row-already-exists-with-non-zero-value leg has no policies analog — pre-seed via raw SQL at a sentinel value (e.g. used_value=17, id=`qu_seed_<resource>`) and prove BOTH the seeded id AND the seeded used_value survive a LockUsage call (this is the ONLY way to prove the discarded INSERT's minted prefix id does not replace the row's id — a regression to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE SET id=EXCLUDED.id would be a catastrophic data-loss path that typed reader projections would not catch); (d) the DOMAIN-violation test casts an arbitrary string to the typed Resource (e.g. `store.QuotaResource("not-a-resource")`) to bypass Go's compile-time check and exercise the DOMAIN quota_resource CHECK at the database layer — surfacing as yerr.CodeConflict through mapWriteError. The 8-new-test shape (row-shape, second-call-byte-identity, peer-row-byte-identity, persisted-used-value, unknown-org-Conflict, unknown-resource-Conflict, tx-rollback, nil-tx-Internal) leaves the pre-existing TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageCreatesAndReturnsCounter in quota_repository_test.go alone as the load-bearing two-call-idempotency probe. Helper symbols introduced: `lockUsageOrFail`, `loadQuotaUsageRow`, `countQuotaUsageRowsForOrg`, `rawQuotaUsageRow`, `quotaUsageTxRollbackSentinel`. Reused symbols from sibling files: `seedOrg` (schema_test.go), `seedQuotaUsage` (usage_test.go), `newStore` (store_test.go) — note `seedQuotaUsage` is the ONLY way to set used_value to a non-zero value through a test fixture because the repository surface has no equivalent (LockUsage cannot increment).
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000055-20260519-014331.log:1559:- Real-quota concurrent oversubscription integration test (BE-0323 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_quota_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0324..0338 — every other quota-dimension PRD story that needs to prove "parallel requests cannot oversubscribe the quota" against the dimension's owning Service.Create call): one Go test, t.Parallel, against testutil.RequireMigratedDB; wires a REAL *quota.Checker over a REAL *store.QuotaRepository + quota.StaticPlanResolver, and a REAL *store.<Resource>Service + REAL *store.AuditRepository, with the only fakes being the Authorizer and JobEnqueuer (recordingAuthorizer / recordingJobs from projectservice_test.go) which always succeed because their failure-path stories are covered elsewhere. Six load-bearing rules: (1) pre-seed quota_usage with used_value=0 for (org, dimension) BEFORE the race so the SELECT FOR UPDATE inside Checker.LockUsage acquires an existing row rather than racing inserts — the lock contract under test is row-lock serialisation, not row-creation; (2) seed quota_policies as scope_kind='plan_default' (not 'organization' override) so the plan-default fallback path inside QuotaRepository.EffectiveLimit is the one exercised — the org-override path has its own dedicated repository test (TestQuotaRepositoryEffectiveLimitPrefersOrgOverride); (3) launch attempts > limit goroutines (BE-0323 uses 20 attempts / limit 5 — 4:1 ratio gives a comfortable margin against any future pool-size or scheduling change without inflating runtime past ~0.5s); each goroutine generates its OWN canonical resource id via domain.MustNewID + a unique canonical slug fmt.Sprintf("p-%02d", i) so a slug conflict cannot disguise itself as a quota rejection; (4) assert successes == limit AND rejections == attempts - limit exactly, NOT >= / <= — the lock contract guarantees an exact split, and a "successes >= limit / <= attempts" range would silently let a regression through; (5) for EVERY rejection, assert all five ExceededDetail fields (Resource, Limit, Requested=1, Reserved==limit, Current==0) because at the moment the lock yields to a losing transaction every winning reservation is already committed — anything less than Reserved==limit means the lock did not serialise — and assert the diagnostic string never echoes any caller input (the i-indexed slug, display name, request id, or correlation id) so a future logging path cannot turn the error into a content channel; (6) post-race assertions cover three independent surfaces of the same invariant: raw count of projects rows for the org (must equal limit), raw count of active quota_reservations rows for the dimension (must equal limit), AND quotaRepo.SumActiveReservations through Store.Read (must equal limit) — the third surface catches a future regression where the table content diverges from the repository's view. Fake-call assertions pin step order at the unit-of-work level: authz.calls == attempts (every transaction reaches authorization) but jobs.calls == limit (rejected transactions never reach the JobEnqueuer because quota fails first and rolls back the whole tx). The dimension constant comes from the public store.QuotaResource* surface (BE-0323 uses store.QuotaResourceProjects) so a future rename surfaces as a compile error rather than a flaky test. NOT applicable to quota dimensions whose enforcement seam lives outside the *Service.Create unit of work (e.g. cpu_millicores / memory_mb / storage_gb where the dimension is summed across child resources — those need a different shape that exercises the dimension's actual enforcement gate). The CapDeploy concurrent_deployments dimension is the load-bearing precedent for "summed across rows" enforcement (DeploymentService.Create at deployment_service.go:209+691) and would clone this template by swapping the Service + dimension while keeping the six rules intact.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000055-20260519-014331.log:1617:- Quota schema (0006_quota_policy_schema): a closed enum reused across tables is a Postgres DOMAIN over text + CHECK (here quota_resource over 14 dimensions, quota_enforcement_mode over hard/soft/metered/disabled); DROP DOMAIN goes in the down migration after the tables. quota_policies holds one limit per (scope, resource) where a row is EITHER a plan_default (text plan, no plans-table FK yet) OR an organization override — enforced by one table CHECK + two partial unique indexes; the checker resolves "org override else plan default". quota_usage is one counter row per (org, resource); quota_reservations are short-lived (amount>0, status active->committed|released|expired, expires_at, settled_at tied to non-active status by CHECK) and expose UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as a composite-FK target; usage_events is append-only (no updated_at/trigger, signed delta) and links reservations via a tenant-safe composite FK. An optional cross-table link inside a tenant uses a plain composite FK (MATCH SIMPLE) — NOT ON DELETE SET NULL, which would null the NOT NULL organization_id.
